#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199326} :activity/id #uuid "e65e5013-8e69-5be7-a53f-8247c1f26c90" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050688 :topic/id #uuid "5f2b42f8-b227-5baa-87a7-72698f765d30" :topic/math-academy-id 4164} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199263 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198366 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198240 :question/id #uuid "7d0da02a-d686-5bb1-ab44-00286ab01783" :question/math-academy-id "e-8690"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffdd4f56-003d-5eed-a60d-ba51c4c86b51" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048162}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446816}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With Inner Critical Points"} :step/id #uuid "c833d29b-43fd-5a3a-83ae-7c9928c184c2" :step/math-academy-id 32292 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199264}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199264 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198367 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198241 :question/id #uuid "9235c493-f092-5f14-b989-be3b4548a90d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8691"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "849449fb-bb08-54a3-b8ea-9f33ddd7a3e5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446818}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Optimization Problems in Context"} :step/id #uuid "b82b3836-2ed6-522e-8a6b-e9301766e38d" :step/math-academy-id 32293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199326 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199484 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the minimum and maximum values of the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x^2+4y^2\n$$\n\nsubject to constraint $g(x,y) = x^2+y^2 \\leq 1.$ In other words, we wish to find the global extrema of $f(x,y)$ defined on the disc of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5a/5ac34145b3911b19eb3775a915e8f0462c78502be6d6292e7682265c86eafce7.png)\n\n  \n\nTo find the extrema, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we find the critical points of $f$ inside the domain $x^2+y^2 < 1.$\n\n- Then, we find the extreme values of $f$ subject to the constraint $x^2+y^2=1$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers.\n\n- Finally, we determine the maximum and minimum values by evaluating $f$ at the candidate points.\n\nRecall that the critical points of a function $f(x,y)$ are the solutions to $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf 0^T.$ Now, since the derivative of $f$ is the transpose of its gradient $\\nabla f,$ the critical points are also given by the solutions to $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0.$\n\nSo, we can find the critical points by solving $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ Note that this point belongs to our domain.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= x^2 + y^2  = 1.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 8y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(2-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(4-\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm 1 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =2$ in the first equation, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4-2)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $\\left(\\pm 1, 0 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm 1, 0), \\qquad (0, \\pm1), \\qquad (0,0).\n$$\n\nNote that we add the critical point $(0,0)$ to the list of the candidates above.\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at these points: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(\\pm 1,0)$ | $(0, \\pm 1)$ | $(0, 0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum of $0$ at the point $(0, 0)$ and a maximum of $4$ at the points $(0, \\pm 1).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7cc22f9f-d1be-570d-acd7-d3f2e0af7a51" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7743 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7bbfb776-26d8-5be7-abb8-cae6bab3294c" :step/math-academy-id 44831 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199327}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199327 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198405 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198047 :question/id #uuid "fb947756-3471-59c8-b3c4-9b21a1a91557" :question/math-academy-id "e-12457"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bbc1e6a-c728-5c5a-84f8-06a92c30a023" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049390}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496295}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With No Inner Critical Points"} :step/id #uuid "0e5ab67d-1254-5853-868a-8e8a117b0032" :step/math-academy-id 44833 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199263}}] :activity/title "Optimizing Multivariable Functions Using Lagrange Multipliers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198494}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496354} :activity/id #uuid "5b35fc89-ed07-5713-aff2-bd03957018fe" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050688 :topic/id #uuid "5f2b42f8-b227-5baa-87a7-72698f765d30" :topic/math-academy-id 4164} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496336 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281543 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0499cbca-daad-57e1-9934-25522e310898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278629} {:answer/id #uuid "08288e31-44a4-5cbd-b685-8435569179d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278665} {:answer/id #uuid "10043f84-5b2d-5986-8b89-d3b961ecb64a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278735} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd71714-856d-5694-afb1-6a391106f770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280278} {:answer/id #uuid "fea569a7-1a86-5d69-9043-62f12b437f4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280818}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278665} :answer-field/id #uuid "c42f751a-ec9a-5f92-8bd2-846f2ed3dd93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e45729b0-5cc3-5497-8a8a-a3af06f9f95a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162780" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-8{y}^{2}$ given that ${x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}\\le 25.$ \n\n*Hint: The critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region ${x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}<25$ is $(0,0).$*" :question/worked-solution "We are given that the critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region's interior is $(0,0).$\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}=25.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=25\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-16y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,10y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -16y &=10\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x(1-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ -2y(8+5\\lambda ) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=25$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2} & =25\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=25$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =25\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(\\pm 5,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}),\\,(5,0),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})$ | $(\\pm 5,0)$ | $(0,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $-40$ | $25$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}),$ and its minimum value is $-40.$"} :step/id #uuid "09e7c588-2af6-5084-a29b-554d52342a46" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496344}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496344 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281547 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f121e3-ccfa-57b8-b6ef-a2e2cd0f3b57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278810} {:answer/id #uuid "65b8a4fe-1d29-5ee2-ad9f-6c0583668e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279516} {:answer/id #uuid "76c3a733-f198-5a7c-a7fb-0c39f1adc2a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279650} {:answer/id #uuid "8bf6ea47-eaeb-5270-89ec-aea8d1830cbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279837} {:answer/id #uuid "944923f9-e97d-5493-8850-5c83f2a0ed3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279911}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279516} :answer-field/id #uuid "797a794d-3c44-5cfd-9959-01ae83cb5934" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b8c68071-089c-5bff-bc03-ba61a8eab03b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162788" :question/problem "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, find the minimum possible sum of three positive numbers whose product is $27.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the three numbers. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x+y+z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\nxyz=27.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=27\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=\\lambda yz \\\\ 1 &=\\lambda xz \\\\ 1 &=\\lambda xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this would give $1=0.$ Therefore, from the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}yz & =xz \\\\ yz-xz & =0 \\\\ (y-x)z & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z>0,$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y=x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xyz & =27 \\\\ x(x)(x) & =27 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =27 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(3,3,3),$ and the minimum value is \n\n$$\nf(3,3,3)=3+3+3=9.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(9,3,1) & =9+3+1 \\\\  & =13 \\\\  & >f(3,3,3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4eb40113-e4c3-5ada-8661-3205e80bd911" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496379}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496354 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496295 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2581670b-66e9-5d37-9926-806360eb8f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495594} {:answer/id #uuid "280f9803-91c8-596b-9d69-7a1755e015d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495597} {:answer/id #uuid "4d7fc2e4-a9ae-57b1-a57d-6f3e9b58edf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495688} {:answer/id #uuid "5b25f91e-47c0-5de9-8785-cc22a420cdde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495717} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a20fa1-2478-557f-919f-c382ef996ee9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495905}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495594} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd28c84e-3672-589b-b259-7ab643cdcbfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a8d5ef6c-fa65-5bc9-a3b7-421525094db4" :question/math-academy-id "q-99271" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=y-3x$ given that  ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 250.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-3,1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=250.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=250\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-3,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 1 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\frac{3}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=-3y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-3y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =250 \\\\ 9{y}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =250 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =25 \\\\ y & =\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-3y=∓15.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-15,5),\\,(15,-5).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-15,5)$ | $(15,-5)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $50$ | $-50$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(-15,5),$ and its maximum value is $50.$"} :step/id #uuid "698d6400-2972-5372-825e-a45e8f947672" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496355}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496355 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362354 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c47837c-91e7-575e-bb0e-eeba6e46e885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361251} {:answer/id #uuid "70afc2df-aa10-56b3-bc23-c23074c89c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361336} {:answer/id #uuid "8c0908b9-98ee-5adc-877d-81b2bba50711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361454} {:answer/id #uuid "9d6f809c-461e-5ba5-8943-29549871ecb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361544} {:answer/id #uuid "b782daff-1cbf-5514-ba1c-f04c88f0cadf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{35}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361656}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361544} :answer-field/id #uuid "69b59c33-2059-5031-b6c9-48e74e90a78e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7eb8a72b-0beb-5d49-b077-ac32cdeca74e" :question/math-academy-id "q-99274" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=8x-3y$ given that $2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}\\le 35.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨8,-3⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=35.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=35\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨8,-3⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,6y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8 &=4\\lambda x \\\\ -3 &=6\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{2}{\\lambda } \\\\ y &=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=-4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-4y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=35,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =35 \\\\ 32{y}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =35 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-4y=∓4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-4,1),\\,(4,-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-4,1)$ | $(4,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-35$ | $35$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(4,-1),$ and its maximum value is $35.$"} :step/id #uuid "69fad04d-c841-5d4c-9ad6-3077f4690f00" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496336}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496379 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281548 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1715e06f-e211-5b5b-b085-4dce3fe10cc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278797} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae4dbb2-6216-51cc-9650-90dd9779ab6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279260} {:answer/id #uuid "b44db878-4ead-5dad-a363-920768dc38d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280179} {:answer/id #uuid "c1fb5f2f-a80f-5006-a9c4-be4750a711f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280294} {:answer/id #uuid "e19f9127-76af-507a-8b89-9b102194e39f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280179} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9a1c281-1dcb-592f-8bb1-786e3c4e7bdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b349e078-f370-57a4-8287-923743beec39" :question/math-academy-id "q-162790" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=x-{y}^{2}$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda x &=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y(1+\\lambda ) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 2,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system gives \n\n$$\n-x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm 2,0),\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(2,0),$ and its maximum value is $2.$"} :step/id #uuid "b9fe1e25-b17e-5b86-bc43-671e9951b1b9"}] :activity/title "Optimizing Multivariable Functions Using Lagrange Multipliers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495498}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512982} :activity/id #uuid "96f786d0-7a4e-5202-b49a-8bf736b34184" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050688 :topic/id #uuid "5f2b42f8-b227-5baa-87a7-72698f765d30" :topic/math-academy-id 4164} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512964 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364648 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f027768c-f196-5e3f-adf4-7554b2177b3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "243\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364650} {:answer/id #uuid "f96459e6-4979-5896-b4e6-40cb50e3fdd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "162\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364651} {:answer/id #uuid "5fe9f1a7-0904-5a92-b9f4-1e7ca2a2d4bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "128\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364652} {:answer/id #uuid "dc20d337-eb61-592e-aeed-865846e45bfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "369\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364653} {:answer/id #uuid "42cfec62-7180-5eb9-8dbb-e33d7fe7ecf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "192\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364654}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364654} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ae58a56-6faf-52c9-9561-c87d35603ea0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9021d181-6a6e-589c-8096-c7ad92921266" :question/math-academy-id "q-99230" :question/problem "A rectangular metal tank without a lid should hold $256\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}$ of liquid. Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the tank's minimum possible surface area." :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the tank's length, width, and height, respectively. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+2xz+2yz,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\nxyz=256.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=256\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨y+2z,x+2z,2x+2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y+2z &=\\lambda yz \\\\ x+2z &=\\lambda xz \\\\ 2x+2y &=\\lambda xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(y+2z) &=\\lambda xyz \\\\ y(x+2z) &=\\lambda xyz \\\\ z(2x+2y) &=\\lambda xyz\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(y+2z) & =y(x+2z) \\\\ xy+2xz & =xy+2yz \\\\ 2xz & =2yz \\\\ 2z(x-y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z>0,$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $x=y=2z.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xyz & =256 \\\\ (2z)(2z)z & =256 \\\\ 4{z}^{3} & =256 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =64 \\\\ z & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(8,8,4),$ and the minimum value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(8,8,4)=8(8)+2(8)(4)+2(8)(4)=192\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(16,16,1) & =(16)(16)+2(16)(1)+2(16)(1) \\\\  & =320 \\\\  & >f(8,8,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1d937a32-c217-5b66-9900-161e09432569" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512965}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512965 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362346 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20c1fa27-09bb-576b-97dd-85dd401dc8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360976} {:answer/id #uuid "681a4a87-3b1d-58fd-b752-c86f6763e6ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361305} {:answer/id #uuid "91c7e663-64a3-56ec-a432-8a3dc5cde46b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361480} {:answer/id #uuid "d174ba97-334a-5124-8075-96cc9ef5a6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5-3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361772} {:answer/id #uuid "dd38c98d-8f6b-5cf8-b355-869da372bc30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361822}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361772} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ce9a233-37ed-5628-bbcf-882c2c09d5d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "85f60f32-4588-5316-bc63-974d85067877" :question/math-academy-id "q-99240" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=5-x+2y$ given that $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-1 &=4\\lambda x \\\\ 2 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\frac{1}{4\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=-\\frac{1}{4}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n  Substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{4}y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=4,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{32}{9} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{4}y=∓\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}),\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ | $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $5+3\\sqrt{2}$ | $5-3\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})\\,,$ and its minimum value is $5-3\\sqrt{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "207020bf-20e1-5c08-9b5a-497678e3c3cc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512988}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512982 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1609399a-f596-5865-ab9c-6aadc92d23db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "54\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360926} {:answer/id #uuid "3a4ae6f5-4806-5c92-95fb-a3ec3dd390a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "42\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361102} {:answer/id #uuid "838bc3f9-5547-5aae-9ce5-c937af91a9a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361421} {:answer/id #uuid "83aec126-3e7b-519c-90b5-432b36990afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361422} {:answer/id #uuid "8c4af1fe-8cbf-5c53-aeb4-2b02ab93e510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361456}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361421} :answer-field/id #uuid "e828baa6-f3ec-5068-b0c9-1c1c3f9d8a66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1ec51af1-dbfa-5fa9-8f74-3c330eb163be" :question/math-academy-id "q-99163" :question/problem "A rectangular parallelepiped has a volume of $125\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}.$ Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, determine the minimum sum of the lengths of its edges." :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the rectangular parallelepiped's length, width, and height, respectively. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=4x+4y+4z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\nxyz=125.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=125\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,4,4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=\\lambda yz \\\\ 4 &=\\lambda xz \\\\ 4 &=\\lambda xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x &=\\lambda xyz \\\\ 4y &=\\lambda xyz \\\\ 4z &=\\lambda xyz\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis gives $x=y=z.$\n\nNow, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xyz & =125 \\\\ x(x)(x) & =125 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =125 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(5,5,5),$ and the minimum value is \n\n$$\nf(5,5,5)=4(5)+4(5)+4(5)=60\\,\\text{in}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(25,5,1) & =4(25)+4(5)+4(1) \\\\  & =121 \\\\  & >f(5,5,5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4b0537ae-3d5e-577c-abe8-31abae4b844f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512964}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512988 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362348 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e208ac1-85a1-5e96-b7fa-52323bca0603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361546} {:answer/id #uuid "a36edd82-90fe-5174-9433-626f33ab66c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361567} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe3af3e-8a8f-5c90-9dc6-fba808b09e58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361694} {:answer/id #uuid "c0a5aea1-7458-5a36-bfcd-ed941a842039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361700} {:answer/id #uuid "ca55e0f9-7162-5f22-b015-8e0165d10253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361751}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361694} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a1f27a1-0eda-517d-a4eb-016e3810f8d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "37949110-2c01-56b7-86c7-c788694289e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-99255" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+10{y}^{2}$ on the region ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ \n\n*Hint*: The critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4$ is $(0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that the critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region's interior is $(0,0).$\n\nNow, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨8x,20y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 20y &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(4-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y(10-\\lambda ) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 2).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 2,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 2),\\,(\\pm 2,0),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 2)$ | $(\\pm 2,0)$ | $(0,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $40$ | $16$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(0,2)$ and $(0,-2),$ and its maximum value is $40.$"} :step/id #uuid "5a12f7cf-20a1-5a1b-867f-1e2e73b2103a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512991}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512991 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "346a190f-bdeb-55f3-9684-8f7a67706bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446752} {:answer/id #uuid "69bcd4e4-7735-5f84-ad2b-2296929423e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446764} {:answer/id #uuid "75897010-0df0-514d-90ad-2e160e86599e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446765} {:answer/id #uuid "ae3016dc-494e-58a7-af10-066972c0ac56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446780} {:answer/id #uuid "df3ed43d-6192-5e7c-985a-432c70a7ae8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446794}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446765} :answer-field/id #uuid "6424ee71-260c-5a9c-9520-211ab5878bce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4c584a36-c1bc-53fc-9ad2-f5f8790f1c21" :question/math-academy-id "q-99270" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=2x-3y$ given that ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}\\le 14.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,-3⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=14.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=14\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,-3⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,6y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -3 &=6\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ y &=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Substituting $x=-2y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=14,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =14 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =14 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-2y=∓2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-2\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}),\\,(2\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-2\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ | $(2\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-7\\sqrt{2}$ | $7\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-2\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}),$ and its minimum value is $-7\\sqrt{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "619b8315-b541-50e9-a444-8d549e33e3d2"}] :activity/title "Optimizing Multivariable Functions Using Lagrange Multipliers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512089}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524088} :activity/id #uuid "4ec68829-0394-5492-8dd6-3692fcfc1d04" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050688 :topic/id #uuid "5f2b42f8-b227-5baa-87a7-72698f765d30" :topic/math-academy-id 4164} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524043 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199018 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "294a6497-3763-569f-bdf9-42356cd00564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "54{\\text{ cm}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361019} {:answer/id #uuid "52b8263c-0e8f-52be-ba17-ae87e84b31f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32{\\text{ cm}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361202} {:answer/id #uuid "52e615b0-5300-5122-b210-2d048d12e825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48{\\text{ cm}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361204} {:answer/id #uuid "96f252cb-20d0-588c-88d0-dd5df5d6f960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24{\\text{ cm}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361510} {:answer/id #uuid "ed3d1ba2-d9d5-5815-a75e-960a8bfa5fbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36{\\text{ cm}}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361890}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361202} :answer-field/id #uuid "62a25a4a-3331-5eca-802d-4fffe8f3048b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c427251e-04fb-5644-a763-9c09169a9227" :question/math-academy-id "q-98104" :question/problem "A rectangular box without a lid has a surface area of $48{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.$ Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum possible volume of the box." :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y$, and $z$ be the box's length, width, and height, respectively. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xyz,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\n2xz+2yz+xy=48.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=2xz+2yz+xy.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=48\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2z+y,2z+x,2x+2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}yz &=\\lambda (2z+y) \\\\ xz &=\\lambda (2z+x) \\\\ xy &=\\lambda (2x+2y)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}xyz &=\\lambda x(2z+y) \\\\ xyz &=\\lambda y(2z+x) \\\\ xyz &=\\lambda z(2x+2y)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this would give $xyz=0,$ which contradicts the constraints. Therefore, from the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2z+y) & =y(2z+x) \\\\ 2xz+xy & =2yz+xy \\\\ xz & =yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z\\ne 0,$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $x=y=2z.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xz+2yz+xy & =48 \\\\ 4{z}^{2}+4{z}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =48 \\\\ 12{z}^{2} & =48 \\\\ z & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(4,4,2),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\nf(4,4,2)=4\\cdot 4\\cdot 2=32\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,2,5.5) & =2\\cdot 2\\cdot 5.5 \\\\  & =22 \\\\  & <f(4,4,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1b2d2a8b-c653-55db-b415-039fee6fd0b6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524096}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524046 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199022 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "940363d2-e432-556a-b0c0-2c42895c1117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198003} {:answer/id #uuid "20e54280-ff88-5af2-8610-6ef05e375392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198004} {:answer/id #uuid "2740310c-d926-5b3d-916c-6755f45ae1b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198005} {:answer/id #uuid "5bdb73f9-d522-5716-a40d-01eb373017ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198006} {:answer/id #uuid "70c9a7e7-b1ad-5149-9703-aa84c63a4445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198007}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198006} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd5a22f3-e62c-5edf-b899-6619cb4a4071" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "73adc7f1-2e5c-592b-b8c9-bd080977f54f" :question/math-academy-id "q-99256" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}$ given that $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16.$ \n\n*Hint: The critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<16$ is $(0,0).$*" :question/worked-solution "We are given that the critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region's interior is $(0,0).$\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=16\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,-6y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x &=8\\lambda x \\\\ -6y &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-2\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y(3+\\lambda ) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=16$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =16\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 4).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=16$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2} & =16\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 2,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 4),\\,(\\pm 2,0),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 4)$ | $(\\pm 2,0)$ | $(0,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $-48$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,\\pm 4),$ and its minimum value is $-48.$"} :step/id #uuid "214df3d6-efb3-56b8-a79a-9d19a508ecbe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524077}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446817 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26df7659-281a-55ad-9717-8a068ed8d410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446748} {:answer/id #uuid "29ba9e62-696e-5df4-a785-8720b21f1d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446750} {:answer/id #uuid "b2fc2520-2872-5f53-b5da-91567e75c405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446782} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3270e4-ba18-50fc-871d-abe0c18290ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446786} {:answer/id #uuid "e475e7cd-dfed-5fbd-ae10-5b0c7895d481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446795}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446782} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ab2ac1e-1760-5933-be14-b547164eb087" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a6e0abbd-abc7-5917-a015-ff462bc321f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-162781" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-4y$ given that $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=4\\lambda x \\\\ -4 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-2\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ \\lambda y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}y=-2\\,⇒\\,y=-4.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+(-4)^{2}=4\\,⇒\\,{x}^{2}=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich has no solutions.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(0,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-8$ | $8$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,2),$ and its minimum value is $-8.$"} :step/id #uuid "8ff88094-3c99-5b7f-a06a-3099afb04e32"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524088 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199016 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17db0ffb-b770-5981-ad25-771eba941c0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197968} {:answer/id #uuid "3b386300-4c24-597f-9204-ff433d9b9e59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197969} {:answer/id #uuid "7b766f86-afe6-5a6b-af92-1c826d2db774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446769} {:answer/id #uuid "9cff40e6-e6af-52b0-9794-92138bab247c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446776} {:answer/id #uuid "ada4dc81-6317-551f-a35f-ec559d59d817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446779}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446779} :answer-field/id #uuid "943b68fc-e739-5e05-8b0d-6d7886409b6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "76ff6072-ae0f-589d-9844-098ccc148f05" :question/math-academy-id "q-98102" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=10+x+y$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical points in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 1 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\ny=x=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $10+\\sqrt{2}$ | $10-\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}),$ and its minimum value is $10-\\sqrt{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "b28a72ae-5376-5f04-8f27-20d4b6edd06e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524043}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524096 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399343 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08059ff9-d83f-540a-a482-2a8f806a18c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "105\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399345} {:answer/id #uuid "40d83586-f330-5ddd-a884-c230dd198024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399346} {:answer/id #uuid "591f3fa0-6797-5340-8094-49d07c9c3d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "216" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399347} {:answer/id #uuid "44d35100-e5d6-5e35-ac5b-f307d1753f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399348} {:answer/id #uuid "1396c469-16f9-566a-aa93-62900910c424" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399349}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399347} :answer-field/id #uuid "25347463-2d58-522d-8979-b8544d1335f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8e5f2d5d-b1b9-503b-9dbd-36de49acff81" :question/math-academy-id "q-99204" :question/problem "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, determine the maximum product of three positive numbers given that the sum of their squares is $108.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be three positive numbers. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xyz,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=108.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=108\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}yz &=2\\lambda x \\\\ xz &=2\\lambda y \\\\ xy &=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}xyz &=2\\lambda {x}^{2} \\\\ xyz &=2\\lambda {y}^{2} \\\\ xyz &=2\\lambda {z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis gives $x=y=z.$\n\nNow, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =108 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =108 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & =108 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =36 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(6,6,6),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\nf(6,6,6)=6\\cdot 6\\cdot 6=216.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(10,2,2) & =10\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =40 \\\\  & <f(6,6,6).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c76a4af5-1fa8-565f-8538-01592aa5b8dd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524046}}] :activity/title "Optimizing Multivariable Functions Using Lagrange Multipliers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523240}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545888} :activity/id #uuid "0ac63a17-8eba-58fd-81f6-c5dfcfca821d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050688 :topic/id #uuid "5f2b42f8-b227-5baa-87a7-72698f765d30" :topic/math-academy-id 4164} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545888 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "839287e3-b143-5415-99de-08e22d1fe7ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197973} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac8fd50-dcb6-5b86-b402-c970434163b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197974} {:answer/id #uuid "fca362ab-06c7-5e0f-a0ea-d44fa5a42449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197975} {:answer/id #uuid "401f21c4-4ada-5f5e-b68f-b4edd51e55ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197976} {:answer/id #uuid "4d850692-8440-57f8-a931-14cb51d6f903" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197977}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197973} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b076631-de18-587b-978a-08fc530f03da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ab0253d0-1db9-532d-bb4a-604c88a16da0" :question/math-academy-id "q-98103" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-4y$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 18.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x-4,2y-4⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(2,2).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=18.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=18\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x-4,2y-4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =1$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 1,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-4 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y-4 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{2}{1-\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{2}{1-\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,x=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ in the condition $g(x,y)=18,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =18 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9 \\\\ x & =\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(3,3),\\,(-3,-3),\\,(2,2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(3,3)$ | $(-3,-3)$ | $(2,2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-6$ | $42$ | $-8$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(-3,-3),$ and its maximum value is $42.$"} :step/id #uuid "0ef9d217-5318-56ef-a70a-b3b1bdd62fac" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546010}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545962 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bacdbdc-a887-5be8-858f-c82cd078c3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446746} {:answer/id #uuid "42ad9543-9de3-59fd-b83c-d669cb4d5f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446755} {:answer/id #uuid "5d8fe052-2121-5c2b-b562-5b0acfc936f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446759} {:answer/id #uuid "8edd449c-a525-5941-972e-625e39f9c583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446773} {:answer/id #uuid "980a9376-7517-58ca-ab4f-a3dde8b299a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446775}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446775} :answer-field/id #uuid "9450a1b5-7499-5e9b-839d-b2e01f9b40ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7c5088ac-9a02-5898-afc8-f9931b4dcdf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-162800" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}$ given that ${x}^{2}+y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+y=2.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=2\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives us the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -4y &=\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y &=-\\frac{\\lambda }{4}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=2$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation gives $y=-\\frac{1}{4},$ and the constraint condition $g(x,y)=2$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4} & =2\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(\\pm \\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(\\pm \\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{4})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-8$ | $\\frac{17}{8}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\pm \\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}),$ and its maximum value is $\\frac{17}{8}.$"} :step/id #uuid "a66e406f-6c08-5429-b179-f50c725ecf90"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545966 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364648 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f027768c-f196-5e3f-adf4-7554b2177b3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "243\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364650} {:answer/id #uuid "f96459e6-4979-5896-b4e6-40cb50e3fdd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "162\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364651} {:answer/id #uuid "5fe9f1a7-0904-5a92-b9f4-1e7ca2a2d4bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "128\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364652} {:answer/id #uuid "dc20d337-eb61-592e-aeed-865846e45bfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "369\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364653} {:answer/id #uuid "42cfec62-7180-5eb9-8dbb-e33d7fe7ecf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "192\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364654}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364654} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ae58a56-6faf-52c9-9561-c87d35603ea0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9021d181-6a6e-589c-8096-c7ad92921266" :question/math-academy-id "q-99230" :question/problem "A rectangular metal tank without a lid should hold $256\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}$ of liquid. Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the tank's minimum possible surface area." :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the tank's length, width, and height, respectively. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+2xz+2yz,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\nxyz=256.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=256\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨y+2z,x+2z,2x+2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y+2z &=\\lambda yz \\\\ x+2z &=\\lambda xz \\\\ 2x+2y &=\\lambda xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(y+2z) &=\\lambda xyz \\\\ y(x+2z) &=\\lambda xyz \\\\ z(2x+2y) &=\\lambda xyz\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(y+2z) & =y(x+2z) \\\\ xy+2xz & =xy+2yz \\\\ 2xz & =2yz \\\\ 2z(x-y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z>0,$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $x=y=2z.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xyz & =256 \\\\ (2z)(2z)z & =256 \\\\ 4{z}^{3} & =256 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =64 \\\\ z & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(8,8,4),$ and the minimum value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(8,8,4)=8(8)+2(8)(4)+2(8)(4)=192\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(16,16,1) & =(16)(16)+2(16)(1)+2(16)(1) \\\\  & =320 \\\\  & >f(8,8,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ad8a33d6-0b0a-5996-8da1-7927904bffbd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546018}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546010 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1609399a-f596-5865-ab9c-6aadc92d23db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "54\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360926} {:answer/id #uuid "3a4ae6f5-4806-5c92-95fb-a3ec3dd390a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "42\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361102} {:answer/id #uuid "838bc3f9-5547-5aae-9ce5-c937af91a9a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361421} {:answer/id #uuid "83aec126-3e7b-519c-90b5-432b36990afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361422} {:answer/id #uuid "8c4af1fe-8cbf-5c53-aeb4-2b02ab93e510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361456}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361421} :answer-field/id #uuid "e828baa6-f3ec-5068-b0c9-1c1c3f9d8a66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1ec51af1-dbfa-5fa9-8f74-3c330eb163be" :question/math-academy-id "q-99163" :question/problem "A rectangular parallelepiped has a volume of $125\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}.$ Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, determine the minimum sum of the lengths of its edges." :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the rectangular parallelepiped's length, width, and height, respectively. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=4x+4y+4z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\nxyz=125.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=125\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,4,4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=\\lambda yz \\\\ 4 &=\\lambda xz \\\\ 4 &=\\lambda xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x &=\\lambda xyz \\\\ 4y &=\\lambda xyz \\\\ 4z &=\\lambda xyz\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis gives $x=y=z.$\n\nNow, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xyz & =125 \\\\ x(x)(x) & =125 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =125 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(5,5,5),$ and the minimum value is \n\n$$\nf(5,5,5)=4(5)+4(5)+4(5)=60\\,\\text{in}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(25,5,1) & =4(25)+4(5)+4(1) \\\\  & =121 \\\\  & >f(5,5,5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "eef0b459-bbf6-5186-8922-6b8989aa4de0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545966}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546018 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281543 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0499cbca-daad-57e1-9934-25522e310898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278629} {:answer/id #uuid "08288e31-44a4-5cbd-b685-8435569179d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278665} {:answer/id #uuid "10043f84-5b2d-5986-8b89-d3b961ecb64a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278735} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd71714-856d-5694-afb1-6a391106f770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280278} {:answer/id #uuid "fea569a7-1a86-5d69-9043-62f12b437f4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280818}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278665} :answer-field/id #uuid "c42f751a-ec9a-5f92-8bd2-846f2ed3dd93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e45729b0-5cc3-5497-8a8a-a3af06f9f95a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162780" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-8{y}^{2}$ given that ${x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}\\le 25.$ \n\n*Hint: The critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region ${x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}<25$ is $(0,0).$*" :question/worked-solution "We are given that the critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region's interior is $(0,0).$\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}=25.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=25\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-16y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,10y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -16y &=10\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x(1-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ -2y(8+5\\lambda ) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=25$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2} & =25\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=25$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =25\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(\\pm 5,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}),\\,(5,0),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5})$ | $(\\pm 5,0)$ | $(0,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $-40$ | $25$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}),$ and its minimum value is $-40.$"} :step/id #uuid "f98db47a-48c0-5d3a-ba0a-051c09b08ff1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545962}}] :activity/title "Optimizing Multivariable Functions Using Lagrange Multipliers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545183}]}
